1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:04,150
从有记载的历史开始，
From the dawn of recorded history

2
00:00:04,260 --> 00:00:05,680
时至今日，
to the present day,

3
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:09,220
人类一直与自身的黑暗本质抗争。
humankind has struggled with its darker nature.

4
00:00:09,330 --> 00:00:12,590
我们知道精神病人可能折磨别人并杀人，
We know that psychopaths can torture and kill

5
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:13,920
而他却没有悔罪之意，
without remorse,

6
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:17,030
但又是什么迫使着看起来正常的人类
but what compels seemingly normal people

7
00:00:17,140 --> 00:00:20,110
实施暴力等残忍的行为？
to commit acts of cruelty and violence?

8
00:00:22,210 --> 00:00:25,830
现今，研究者们正在揭示
Today, researchers are uncovering the hidden forces

9
00:00:25,950 --> 00:00:28,040
燃起我们内心恶魔的隐藏力量，
that inflame our inner demons,

10
00:00:28,150 --> 00:00:31,740
并寻找方法中和我们最致命的的欲望
looking for ways to neutralize our deadliest urges

11
00:00:31,850 --> 00:00:34,540
来改变人类本性。
and change human nature.

12
00:00:34,660 --> 00:00:37,890
我们可以消除邪恶吗？
Can we eliminate evil?

13
00:00:43,030 --> 00:00:48,100
《同摩根·弗里曼一起穿越虫洞》
空间、时间、生命本身
Space, time, life itself.

14
00:00:49,870 --> 00:00:54,730
宇宙的秘密，尽在穿越虫洞
The secrets of the cosmos lie through the wormhole.

15
00:01:00,740 --> 00:01:05,470
我们可以消除邪恶吗？

16
00:01:07,740 --> 00:01:11,470
几乎没有人认为自己是邪恶的，
Few people consider themselves evil,

17
00:01:11,580 --> 00:01:15,800
但邪恶又是生活中又不可避免的部分。
yet evil seems an inescapable part of life.

18
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:19,370
为什么会出现这种情况？
The mystery is -- why?

19
00:01:19,490 --> 00:01:22,580
几千年来，我们谴责恶魔--
For millennia, we blamed the devil --

20
00:01:22,690 --> 00:01:27,080
一种黑暗的生物催使我们做出可怕的事。
a creature of darkness that made us do terrible things.

21
00:01:27,190 --> 00:01:33,390
现在大多数基督徒认为撒旦是恶魔的代表。
Today, most Christians believe Satan is just a symbol.

22
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:36,890
心理学家和脑科学家向我们展示
Psychologists and brain scientists have shown us

23
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:42,030
我们所惧怕的邪恶，就出自于我们自己。
that the evil we fear comes from within ourselves.

24
00:01:42,140 --> 00:01:44,830
邪恶会永存于我们自身，
Will it always be there,

25
00:01:44,940 --> 00:01:49,180
还是科学会找到其根基并将其消灭？
or can science find its roots and destroy it?

26
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:56,910
还是9岁的那年，我搬回了芝加哥。
When I was about 9 years old, we moved back to Chicago.

27
00:01:57,020 --> 00:02:02,060
因为在邻居中我是新人，于是就成了被欺负的目标。
Being new in the neighborhood, I became the target of a bully.

28
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:10,670
有一天，我实在受够了。
One day, I decided enough was enough.

29
00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:15,660
但是看着他倒在地上，
But as I watched him lying there on the ground,

30
00:02:15,770 --> 00:02:20,670
我发现自己无法享受这种意外的胜利。
I found I just couldn't savor my unexpected victory.

31
00:02:20,780 --> 00:02:25,310
我在想，是什么让这个孩子变得如此卑劣？
I wondered, what made this kid so mean?

32
00:02:25,420 --> 00:02:29,720
有些人出生就是坏的吗？
Are some people just born bad?

33
00:02:40,530 --> 00:02:41,860
停！
And cut!

34
00:02:41,970 --> 00:02:44,460
接下来我需要你做的
So, what I need you to do for the next one

35
00:02:44,570 --> 00:02:46,230
把他的手指再用力折一点。
is really curl out a bit more.

36
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:48,400
我需要看看再痛一点的情况。
I need to see more pain in there.

37
00:02:48,510 --> 00:02:50,100
在阿姆斯特丹，
In Amsterdam,

38
00:02:50,210 --> 00:02:52,430
神经学家Christian Keysers
neuroscientist Christian Keysers

39
00:02:52,550 --> 00:02:55,640
正在寻找人类残忍的源头。
is looking for the source of human cruelty.

40
00:02:55,750 --> 00:02:59,410
好的，我真的想...就像我在打架... 【无上下文不好翻译啊...】
Okay, so I really want to seem as I'm fighting, where...

41
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:01,580
Christian研究了移情作用--
Christian investigates empathy --

42
00:03:01,690 --> 00:03:04,480
我们的一种鉴别并对别人
our ability to identify and respond

43
00:03:04,590 --> 00:03:07,080
想的或感受的起反应的能力。
to what someone else thinks or feels.

44
00:03:07,190 --> 00:03:08,980
好的，不错！
Okay, that was good.

45
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:12,920
为了找出移情作用在大脑中的工作原理，
To find out how empathy works in our brains,

46
00:03:13,030 --> 00:03:15,860
Christian制作了一些疼痛行为的短片，
Christian makes short films of painful acts

47
00:03:15,970 --> 00:03:17,790
让受试者观看。
to screen for test subjects.

48
00:03:17,910 --> 00:03:19,960
我们要严格控制这种刺激，
We need very controlled stimuli,

49
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:23,200
只出现两到三秒的疼痛，
where we just see two or three seconds of pain,

50
00:03:23,310 --> 00:03:25,800
然后我们要重复许多组，
and we need to repeat many of them,

51
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:27,880
这样就可以让受试者认为短片中的是自己。
which is why we need to make them ourselves.

52
00:03:30,420 --> 00:03:33,540
这些受试者都是我们的研究生和博士后，
So our actors are typically our graduate students and postdocs,

53
00:03:33,660 --> 00:03:35,580
因为他们知道自己在做的什么项目，
because they know what they're doing

54
00:03:35,690 --> 00:03:37,850
这样就能承受一些疼痛。
and they can take some pain.

55
00:03:37,960 --> 00:03:41,120
Christian把他的“虐待短片”
Christian screens his torture films

56
00:03:41,230 --> 00:03:43,870
在世界上从未有过的“剧场”中播出。
in a theater unlike any on earth.

57
00:03:46,770 --> 00:03:50,660
功能磁共振成像(一种大脑成像技术)仪器中的磁传感器
Magnetic sensors inside this fMRI machine

58
00:03:50,770 --> 00:03:53,700
可以看到受试者的大脑深处，
will peer deep into this man's brain,

59
00:03:53,810 --> 00:03:58,300
并展示出当他出现移情作用时的大脑活跃区域。
showing which areas are active when he experiences empathy.

60
00:03:58,420 --> 00:04:00,900
好，我会给你这个按钮盒。
Okay, so I'll give you this button box.

61
00:04:01,020 --> 00:04:03,670
我想让你做的是，
What I want you to do is, each time,

62
00:04:03,790 --> 00:04:07,290
评估出每次试验中你的感受。
to rate what you felt in this particular trial.

63
00:04:11,430 --> 00:04:14,390
首先，Christian记录下了受试者看到
First, Christian records what happens in the subject's brain

64
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:16,600
别人疼痛时自身大脑的反应。
when he sees someone else in pain.

65
00:04:21,540 --> 00:04:23,690
然后他测量了受试者
Then he measures what happens

66
00:04:23,810 --> 00:04:27,380
自身感到疼痛后大脑的表现。
when the subject experiences pain first-hand.

67
00:04:31,020 --> 00:04:34,270
现在他比较两种结果，
Now he compares the brain scans.

68
00:04:34,390 --> 00:04:37,440
这里我们研究的移情作用...
So, the emotional empathy we've been studying here,

69
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:40,210
你主要能看到大脑有一部分活跃，
you would mainly see in parts of your brain

70
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:42,910
这部分并不在你的大脑的表面，
that are not on the surface of your brain,

71
00:04:43,030 --> 00:04:46,420
而是在更深一点的脑岛，
but inside of the insula that's a little bit deeper here,

72
00:04:46,530 --> 00:04:49,890
就在你两个脑半球的中线。
and, really, in the midline between your two hemispheres.

73
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,990
好吧，你在这里看到的基本上是红色的区域--
Okay, so what you're seeing here is basically in red --

74
00:04:53,100 --> 00:04:54,720
表明受试者被打的时候
the brain activity that happened

75
00:04:54,840 --> 00:04:58,130
这部分大脑区域变得活跃。
while we were hitting the subjects in the scanner.

76
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:01,870
然后是这里，你看到的是两个情感大脑区域，
And then here, you see two of the emotional-brain regions.

77
00:05:01,980 --> 00:05:05,900
它们加强了你感受到的不快。
They really add this feeling of unpleasantness to what you feel.

78
00:05:06,020 --> 00:05:09,740
它们就像是在告诉你“哦！我不喜欢这样！”之类的。
So, they're telling you, kind of, "Ouch, I don't like that."

79
00:05:09,850 --> 00:05:13,040
然后我们在底部看到的，
And so what we're seeing here, in the bottom,

80
00:05:13,160 --> 00:05:15,210
当受试者看到别人疼痛时
the brain activity that happens

81
00:05:15,330 --> 00:05:18,880
大脑的活跃区域。
while the subject was watching somebody else's pain.

82
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:22,590
所有的情感区域甚至更多的都被激活，
All of these more emotional areas get reactivated,

83
00:05:22,700 --> 00:05:26,190
就好像受试者自己感受到疼痛一样。
as if the subject had been feeling pain himself.

84
00:05:26,310 --> 00:05:29,600
无论何时你看到别人遭受痛苦，
Whenever you see the pain of somebody else,

85
00:05:29,710 --> 00:05:32,200
你自身也会有如此感觉。
you will share it inside of yourself.

86
00:05:32,310 --> 00:05:35,130
那些人似乎成了你自己的一部分，
The other person becomes part of yourself.

87
00:05:35,250 --> 00:05:38,970
你不仅看到的是别人的疼痛，
The pain of others is not just something you see out there.

88
00:05:39,090 --> 00:05:41,110
这种感觉也会发自你自身，
It basically comes inside of you,

89
00:05:41,220 --> 00:05:43,540
让你同时也感受到疼痛。
and it becomes your pain, as well.

90
00:05:43,660 --> 00:05:46,280
通过对上百人的试验，
After screening hundreds of people,

91
00:05:46,390 --> 00:05:49,210
Christian认为移情作用是
Christian believes that empathy is hard-wired

92
00:05:49,330 --> 00:05:52,150
我们几乎所有大脑与生俱来的，
into nearly all of our brains,

93
00:05:52,260 --> 00:05:54,790
但每个人的又有区别。
but it is not distributed equally.

94
00:05:54,900 --> 00:05:56,950
这儿有一个移情作用曲线，
There is a curve of empathy.

95
00:05:57,070 --> 00:05:59,990
一些人的移情作用非常强，
Some people are extremely empathetic,

96
00:06:00,100 --> 00:06:02,530
又有一些人基本感觉不到什么。
others feel almost nothing.

97
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:04,460
就像看一部浪漫电影，
Think of a romantic movie.

98
00:06:04,570 --> 00:06:08,230
大多数人都会融入荧幕中的情感，
Most of us get caught up in the emotions on screen.

99
00:06:08,340 --> 00:06:10,100
但是，但是为什么这样？
But -- but why?

100
00:06:10,210 --> 00:06:12,240
这件事必须了结，
This has to be the end.

101
00:06:12,350 --> 00:06:14,920
永别了。
Goodbye.

102
00:06:17,920 --> 00:06:21,490
但是对于一小部分人，这部电影就像这样：
But for a few of us, it plays like this.

103
00:06:22,990 --> 00:06:26,120
具有较弱移情作用的人看到听到的会有不同，
People with low empathy see and hear things differently

104
00:06:26,230 --> 00:06:29,420
因为他们大脑的工作方式不同。
because their brains work differently.

105
00:06:29,530 --> 00:06:32,590
信息在大多数大脑中的流动，
Information flows through most brains

106
00:06:32,700 --> 00:06:36,430
像船只在阿姆斯特丹的运河中穿梭一样。
like boats move along the canals of Amsterdam.

107
00:06:36,540 --> 00:06:38,100
但是在一些人的大脑中，
But in some brains,

108
00:06:38,210 --> 00:06:42,700
这种流动被狭窄或阻塞的通道所阻碍，
that movement is impeded by narrow, or blocked, channels.

109
00:06:42,810 --> 00:06:44,770
那么如果你在刚才那边，
So, if you imagine that back there,

110
00:06:44,880 --> 00:06:46,870
你就处于视觉脑区，
you would have the visual-brain areas

111
00:06:46,990 --> 00:06:48,570
你能看到别人身上发生了什么，
that see what happens to others,

112
00:06:48,690 --> 00:06:51,310
接着到了那里，你就处于情感区域，
and down there, you would have the emotional areas

113
00:06:51,420 --> 00:06:52,850
那里用来正常感受你的痛苦。
that normally feel your pain.

114
00:06:52,960 --> 00:06:56,020
然后我们可以考虑下是什么在根本上
And we think that what makes the difference, basically,

115
00:06:56,130 --> 00:06:59,350
造成了一个移情作用强的人与弱的人之间的区别。
between a very empathic person and a less empathic person

116
00:06:59,470 --> 00:07:00,990
正是将信息从脑视觉区域
is just the size of the canal

117
00:07:01,100 --> 00:07:04,520
带到脑情感区域
that brings the information from the visual part of your brain

118
00:07:04,640 --> 00:07:05,890
的“运河”的宽度决定的， to the emotional part,

119
00:07:06,010 --> 00:07:08,800
这就决定了你如何去体会别人的痛苦。
in which you will share the pain of other people.

120
00:07:08,910 --> 00:07:12,170
那么我们一般人梦魇中的怪物--
And what of the monsters of our nightmares --

121
00:07:12,280 --> 00:07:15,810
看起来外表正常，但内心扭曲的...
the psychopathic killers who look normal on the outside...

122
00:07:17,150 --> 00:07:19,540
变态杀人狂又是什么样呢？
...but are twisted on the inside?

123
00:07:19,650 --> 00:07:23,710
通常认为精神病人不具有移情作用，
It is often said that psychopaths have no empathy,

124
00:07:23,820 --> 00:07:27,250
正是这种移情作用的缺失导致了他们的邪恶。
and this lack of empathy makes them evil.

125
00:07:27,360 --> 00:07:30,250
他们会去折磨别人并杀人，
They are able to torture and kill

126
00:07:30,360 --> 00:07:33,920
因为他们无法与别人的感情联系起来。
because they can't relate to other people.

127
00:07:34,030 --> 00:07:35,590
但Christian不这么认为。
Christian disagrees.

128
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:39,330
我认为通过对精神病人研究的发现
Well, I think the finding that surprised us most

129
00:07:39,440 --> 00:07:41,830
会让我们非常吃惊。
was actually the study on psychopaths.

130
00:07:41,940 --> 00:07:44,100
这些以前的想法非常简单，
We went in there with a simple idea

131
00:07:44,210 --> 00:07:46,370
邪恶的人，比如精神病人，
that evil people, like psychopaths,

132
00:07:46,480 --> 00:07:47,870
不具有移情作用，
would just lack empathy,

133
00:07:47,980 --> 00:07:50,340
但我们在这项研究中发现，
and what we actually saw in this study

134
00:07:50,450 --> 00:07:53,140
他们会变得罪恶是因为更复杂的作用，
is that what makes them evil is more complex.

135
00:07:53,250 --> 00:07:56,240
他们并不是缺乏移情作用--
It's not that they lack the capacity for empathy --

136
00:07:56,360 --> 00:07:59,050
他们只是不会自主引发这种作用。
they just don't use it spontaneously.

137
00:07:59,160 --> 00:08:02,150
但是如果他们想，
But if they want to, because, for instance,

138
00:08:02,260 --> 00:08:03,680
出于某中目的，
it serves the purpose

139
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:07,420
比如骗走别人所有的钱，
of fooling somebody into giving them all their money,

140
00:08:07,530 --> 00:08:09,620
这个时候他们的移情作用
then they're quite able to empathize

141
00:08:09,740 --> 00:08:11,940
和正常人的一样。
and really get in to people's minds.

142
00:08:13,810 --> 00:08:17,200
所以移情作用并不是邪恶的唯一原因。
So empathy is not everything.

143
00:08:17,310 --> 00:08:19,830
为了防止陷入邪恶，
To keep from falling into evil,

144
00:08:19,950 --> 00:08:22,970
我们还需要一个道德体系来指导我们的行为--
we also need a moral system to guide our behavior --

145
00:08:23,080 --> 00:08:27,270
这套行为准则帮助我们融入社会，
a code of conduct that helps us fit in to society

146
00:08:27,390 --> 00:08:29,880
并以一种非毁灭式的方法行事。
and act in a non-destructive way.

147
00:08:29,990 --> 00:08:34,310
耶鲁大学的科学家Karen Wynn和Paul Bloom
Scientists Karen Wynn and Paul Bloom of Yale University

148
00:08:34,430 --> 00:08:38,950
认为我们出生时就具有这种道德体系。
believe that moral code may be written into us at birth.

149
00:08:39,070 --> 00:08:42,120
你嘴里吃的可是很大的一只鸭子啊。
That is a lot of duck you're fitting in your mouth.

150
00:08:42,240 --> 00:08:46,490
我已经针对婴儿研究了刚好20多年，
I've been studying babies now for just a little over 20 years.

151
00:08:46,610 --> 00:08:50,060
我越多地观察他们，
The more that I see of them,

152
00:08:50,180 --> 00:08:53,740
他们看起来就越复杂。
the more complex they become.

153
00:08:53,850 --> 00:08:57,270
他们内心有很多的事情，
There is a lot going on in there,

154
00:08:57,380 --> 00:09:01,880
对于智慧生命来说，
and it's far more rich and complex

155
00:09:01,990 --> 00:09:06,210
他们远比我们以前认为的更丰富更复杂。
of a mental life than we had ever thought.

156
00:09:06,330 --> 00:09:08,020
通过对婴儿的研究，
By studying babies,

157
00:09:08,130 --> 00:09:11,950
你可以看到被文化污染之前的人类，
you get to see humans before they're contaminated by culture,

158
00:09:12,070 --> 00:09:15,090
包括电视节目，还有很多的社交活动，
by television, by a lot of social interactions,

159
00:09:15,200 --> 00:09:16,560
以及性和浪漫。
by sex and romance.

160
00:09:16,670 --> 00:09:20,060
某种程度上来说，你能看到人类最纯洁的形式，
You get to see humans, in some sense, in their purest form,

161
00:09:20,170 --> 00:09:22,490
然后你会问，“到底什么才是我们的本性？”
and you could ask, "what's our natures?

162
00:09:22,610 --> 00:09:24,660
“我们是善良的？还是残忍的？
"Are we kind? Are we cruel?

163
00:09:24,780 --> 00:09:29,130
我们具有道德智能？我们能够分辨正邪吗？”
Are we morally intelligent? Can we tell good from evil?"

164
00:09:29,250 --> 00:09:31,100
我和同事在这里做的工作
And the work I'm doing here with my colleague

165
00:09:31,220 --> 00:09:33,140
表明在人类成长早期
suggests that very early on

166
00:09:33,250 --> 00:09:35,370
就已经具有一些基本道德感--
there's some fundamental moral sense --

167
00:09:35,490 --> 00:09:38,120
一些我们每个人都天生的道德本能。
some moral instinct that's present in all of us.

168
00:09:40,690 --> 00:09:44,480
你如何向婴儿表述道德问题？
How do you pose moral questions to a baby?

169
00:09:44,600 --> 00:09:47,450
Karen设计了一种道德游戏，
Karen devised a kind of morality play

170
00:09:47,570 --> 00:09:49,720
让婴儿们观察并加以判断。
for babies to watch and judge.

171
00:09:49,840 --> 00:09:52,190
我们让婴儿观看玩偶表演
We show babies a little puppet show

172
00:09:52,300 --> 00:09:55,460
表演中一个玩偶试图打开一个盒子，
in which this one puppet is trying to open a box,

173
00:09:55,580 --> 00:09:57,430
它试了一次又一次，
and he's trying and he's trying

174
00:09:57,540 --> 00:10:00,270
可它单独无法打开，
and he just can't quite get it on his own,

175
00:10:00,380 --> 00:10:01,800
这时候另一个玩偶过来，
and another puppet comes along

176
00:10:01,920 --> 00:10:04,370
抓住盒盖的另一边帮助它打开了盒子。
and grabs the other side of the box lid and helps him open it.

177
00:10:04,480 --> 00:10:06,610
然后让婴儿们接着观看，
They then see the little puppet.

178
00:10:06,720 --> 00:10:08,880
这个玩偶又在尝试打开盒子，
He's trying again to open the box,

179
00:10:08,990 --> 00:10:11,110
一个不同的玩偶过来，
and a different puppet comes along

180
00:10:11,230 --> 00:10:14,020
跳在盒盖上，盒子一下就关上了。
and jumps on top of the box lid, slams it shut.

181
00:10:14,130 --> 00:10:15,320
哦！
Oh!

182
00:10:15,430 --> 00:10:17,390
这样我们给婴儿们准备的问题是，
And so our question to the babies is,

183
00:10:17,500 --> 00:10:19,820
“孩子们，你们对于两种不同的角色
"Babies, do you have any different feelings

184
00:10:19,930 --> 00:10:21,490
“有什么不同的感觉--
"towards these two characters --

185
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:24,690
“一个以好心的方式帮助别人打开盒子，
"towards the one who helped, in a nice fashion, open the box,

186
00:10:24,810 --> 00:10:27,160
“而另一个则是非常粗鲁的
"and towards this other, who just really, quite rudely,

187
00:10:27,270 --> 00:10:28,500
“把盒子关上，
"slams it down

188
00:10:28,610 --> 00:10:30,330
不让这只玩偶把盒子打开？”
and foils this guy's attempts to get in to the box?"

189
00:10:30,440 --> 00:10:32,430
你喜欢哪一个呢？
Which one do you like?

190
00:10:32,550 --> 00:10:34,130
我们发现，
And we find that, very reliably,

191
00:10:34,250 --> 00:10:37,970
甚至大概5到6个月大的婴儿，
babies, even as young as five and six months of age,

192
00:10:38,080 --> 00:10:41,840
会喜欢上帮助别人的玩偶。
will reach towards and reach for the helpful puppet.

193
00:10:41,950 --> 00:10:45,440
这个！OK，不错！
That one! Okay, good job!

194
00:10:45,560 --> 00:10:48,610
Layla选了那个好心的玩偶。
Layla has chosen the good puppet.

195
00:10:48,730 --> 00:10:51,220
大概80%到95%的孩子会这么做。
Between 80% and 95% of babies do.

196
00:10:51,330 --> 00:10:54,450
Paul和Karen认为这是一种标志，
Paul and Karen believe that this is a sign

197
00:10:54,560 --> 00:10:57,390
标志着孩子们倾向于善良
that babies are drawn towards kindness

198
00:10:57,500 --> 00:11:00,160
并远离反社会行为。
and away from antisocial behavior.

199
00:11:00,270 --> 00:11:03,390
但是既然大多数人生来即善，
But if most of us are born good,

200
00:11:03,510 --> 00:11:07,130
为什么还有那么多人变坏呢？
why do some of us turn out so bad?

201
00:11:07,240 --> 00:11:08,970
有很多种方式
Well, there's all sorts of ways

202
00:11:09,080 --> 00:11:11,000
都会使我们的正义感扭曲。
in which our sense of good could get perverted.

203
00:11:11,110 --> 00:11:12,400
如果你在一种使你轻视别人
If you're brought up in a culture

204
00:11:12,520 --> 00:11:14,170
的文化中成长起来，
which teaches you to be dismissive of others,

205
00:11:14,280 --> 00:11:17,980
而且还奖励自私自利与不良行为，
which rewards selfishness, which rewards bad behavior,

206
00:11:18,090 --> 00:11:20,880
你的移情作用就会变得迟钝。
your sense of empathy could be blunted.

207
00:11:20,990 --> 00:11:24,820
我们拥有天生的道德感，但非常脆弱。
So we have this built-in morality, but it's fragile.

208
00:11:24,930 --> 00:11:27,750
一旦我们走向黑暗--
Once we descend into darkness --

209
00:11:27,860 --> 00:11:30,620
暴力袭击、奸淫、谋杀--
assault, rape, murder --

210
00:11:30,730 --> 00:11:32,190
就迷失了自我，
are we lost,

211
00:11:32,300 --> 00:11:36,460
还是可以克制导致邪恶的冲动？
or can the impulses that lead to evil be squelched?

212
00:11:36,570 --> 00:11:38,130
这个人认为可以。
This man thinks so.

213
00:11:38,240 --> 00:11:41,330
他认为我们可以强化我们的大脑
He believes we can strengthen our brains

214
00:11:41,450 --> 00:11:43,950
来制服我们内在的邪恶。
and crush the evil within.

215
00:11:44,560 --> 00:11:49,020
每个人类的内心深处都是野兽--
Deep inside every human being is an animal --

216
00:11:49,170 --> 00:11:52,510
一只以生存为唯一目的的生物。
a creature whose only goal is survival.

217
00:11:52,810 --> 00:11:56,230
大多数人可以包容这种野性，
Most of us can contain the animal within,

218
00:11:56,340 --> 00:12:00,950
但我们也知道人们会屈从于他们卑劣的冲动。
but we all know people who yield to their baser impulses.

219
00:12:01,190 --> 00:12:05,740
有时他们的行为会造成严重后果，
Sometimes their actions have terrible consequences.

220
00:12:05,860 --> 00:12:09,650
我们谴责那些实施罪恶行为的人，
We blame these people for their evil acts,

221
00:12:09,760 --> 00:12:12,950
但是他们真的有机会选择么？
but do they really have a choice?

222
00:12:13,060 --> 00:12:16,120
经过数年对人类思维的测试，
After years of probing the human mind,

223
00:12:16,230 --> 00:12:18,390
神经学家David Eagleman
neuroscientist David Eagleman

224
00:12:18,500 --> 00:12:21,230
就职于贝勒医学院，
of the Baylor College of Medicine

225
00:12:21,340 --> 00:12:24,400
他得到了一个惊人的结论--
has come to a startling conclusion --

226
00:12:24,510 --> 00:12:28,670
如果有一点运气不佳，我们都会变成恶魔。
with a little bad luck, we could all become monsters.

227
00:12:28,780 --> 00:12:31,100
从任何轴向测试大脑，
Along any axis you measure brains,

228
00:12:31,220 --> 00:12:34,710
无论是移情作用、智力还是攻击性，
whether that's empathy or intelligence or aggression,

229
00:12:34,820 --> 00:12:36,640
你会发现负责这些作用的区域分布广泛。
you find a big distribution.

230
00:12:36,750 --> 00:12:39,080
并不是所有的大脑都是一样的，
Not all brains are the same.

231
00:12:39,190 --> 00:12:41,380
你是你自己的大脑，
You are your brain,

232
00:12:41,490 --> 00:12:45,320
你的大脑是精细脆弱又高度复杂的设备。
and your brain is a delicate, highly complex apparatus.

233
00:12:45,430 --> 00:12:49,490
受伤或疾病会导致其化学平衡
Injury or disease can alter its chemical balance

234
00:12:49,600 --> 00:12:51,520
和物理完整性的改变，
and physical integrity,

235
00:12:51,640 --> 00:12:54,530
而这种变化又会改变你的人格。
which can alter your personality.

236
00:12:54,640 --> 00:12:57,560
如果你在一次意外中伤到了你的拇指，
If you were to damage your thumb in an accident,

237
00:12:57,680 --> 00:13:00,000
这不会改变你的人格，
that wouldn't change you as a person,

238
00:13:00,110 --> 00:13:02,570
但是如果大脑中相当大的一块区域
but if you damage an equivalently sized

239
00:13:02,680 --> 00:13:04,000
受到损伤，
chunk of brain tissue,

240
00:13:04,120 --> 00:13:06,410
就会改变你的风险承受力，
that can change your risk taking,

241
00:13:06,520 --> 00:13:07,940
以及你做的决定，
your decision making,

242
00:13:08,050 --> 00:13:10,820
或许甚至会让你成为一个谋杀犯。
and even, perhaps, whether you become a murderer.

243
00:13:15,390 --> 00:13:16,680
1966年，
In 1966,

244
00:13:16,800 --> 00:13:20,090
Charles Whitman爬上了位于奥斯汀的德州大学的
Charles Whitman climbed to the top of the tower

245
00:13:20,200 --> 00:13:22,020
一个钟楼，
on the U.T. Austin campus,

246
00:13:22,130 --> 00:13:24,960
无选择的射击了48个人。
and he indiscriminantly shot 48 people.

247
00:13:25,070 --> 00:13:28,030
此次事件的恐怖之处就在于
The only thing that matched the horror of this event

248
00:13:28,140 --> 00:13:29,900
一切都是毫无预料的。
was the unexpected nature of it.

249
00:13:30,010 --> 00:13:31,800
他的生平中并没有
There was nothing in his history

250
00:13:31,910 --> 00:13:34,700
什么动机让他怎么做。
that would have predicted this sort of behavior.

251
00:13:34,810 --> 00:13:38,100
他是一个工程系学生，也是一名银行出纳员。
He was an engineering student. He worked as a bank teller.

252
00:13:38,220 --> 00:13:41,040
他与他的妻子和岳母住在一起。
He lived with his wife and his mother-in-law.

253
00:13:41,150 --> 00:13:42,840
那么该如何解释这种情况？
So what could explain this?

254
00:13:42,950 --> 00:13:46,550
在他的自杀遗嘱上写道：“当这一切都结束后，
Well, in his suicide note, he said, "When this is all over,

255
00:13:46,660 --> 00:13:48,980
希望能够对我有一个尸检。”
I want an autopsy to be performed."

256
00:13:49,090 --> 00:13:50,820
人们在他的脑子里发现了
And what they found in his brain

257
00:13:50,930 --> 00:13:53,180
一个核桃大小的肿瘤，
was a tumor about the size of a walnut,

258
00:13:53,300 --> 00:13:55,820
肿瘤挤压了大脑的
and it was pressing on a region of the brain

259
00:13:55,930 --> 00:13:57,090
一个叫做“杏仁核(杏仁体)”的部分，
called the amygdala,

260
00:13:57,200 --> 00:13:59,560
这部分关系着恐惧和进攻性。
which is involved in fear and aggression.

261
00:13:59,670 --> 00:14:03,160
杏仁体是我们的情感中心--
The amygdala is the center of emotion --

262
00:14:03,270 --> 00:14:05,730
也是我们原始冲动的源头。
the source of our primal desires.

263
00:14:05,840 --> 00:14:08,800
它所产生的冲动由“额叶”
It is held in check by the frontal lobes

264
00:14:08,910 --> 00:14:12,700
和自控中心--“颞叶”检查，
and the temporal lobes -- the centers of self-control.

265
00:14:12,820 --> 00:14:16,670
我们所有人都有邪恶的潜意识，并不停的经过审查，
We all have subconscious demons that we keep in check,

266
00:14:16,790 --> 00:14:21,310
但如果额叶或颞叶遭受损伤，
but when the frontal or temporal lobes are compromised,

267
00:14:21,430 --> 00:14:23,380
人们就会产生让人吃惊的行为--
startling behaviors can emerge --

268
00:14:23,490 --> 00:14:26,750
我们称为邪恶的行为。
behaviors we call evil.

269
00:14:26,860 --> 00:14:30,820
这或许就是为什么Charles Whitman这么做的原因。
This is what probably happened to Charles Whitman.

270
00:14:30,940 --> 00:14:34,930
他感觉到了自己脑部有些问题，
He sensed that something was wrong with his brain.

271
00:14:35,040 --> 00:14:39,430
他无法控制自己的暴力冲动。
He could no longer control his violent impulses.

272
00:14:39,540 --> 00:14:42,370
这就是人类大脑中的一场战斗--
And one of the battles that humans have to fight

273
00:14:42,480 --> 00:14:45,240
短期决策和长期决策的对抗。
is short-term versus long-term decision making.

274
00:14:45,350 --> 00:14:47,810
我们有使自己满足的冲动，
We have impulses that we want to gratify,

275
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:49,680
我们还有长远考虑，
and we have longer-term thinking

276
00:14:49,790 --> 00:14:51,910
可以抑制那些冲动。
that try to squelch those impulses.

277
00:14:52,020 --> 00:14:53,550
就比如说我在考虑
Let's say that I'm considering

278
00:14:53,660 --> 00:14:55,910
用这块砖砸那扇窗户--
throwing this brick through this window --

279
00:14:56,030 --> 00:14:57,680
我的一部分想让我这么做，
part of me maybe wants to do it,

280
00:14:57,800 --> 00:14:59,750
而我的另一部分认为这是一种违法行为，
part of me feels it's an illegal act

281
00:14:59,860 --> 00:15:02,250
如果这么做了我就会被逮捕，陷入麻烦之中，
and I'll get caught and I'll get in trouble.

282
00:15:02,370 --> 00:15:05,260
这就像是两种想法之间的扳手腕。
And it's an arm wrestle between these different things.

283
00:15:05,370 --> 00:15:07,440
而有些人冲动部分比另一部分强。
Some people are better at this than others.

284
00:15:13,280 --> 00:15:16,570
David认为我们可以通过一点练习
David believes we can strengthen our willpower

285
00:15:16,680 --> 00:15:18,070
加强自己的意志力，
with a little workout.

286
00:15:18,180 --> 00:15:21,940
和神经科学家Stephen Laconte一起，
Together with neuroscientist Stephen Laconte,

287
00:15:22,060 --> 00:15:26,610
他正在测试一个叫做“前额叶健身房”的东西。
he is testing something called "the prefrontal gym."

288
00:15:26,730 --> 00:15:28,520
当然这里并没有运动器械，
There are no treadmills here,

289
00:15:28,630 --> 00:15:32,320
而是让人们进入一台脑部扫描仪。
just a scanner that lets people see how their brains respond

290
00:15:32,430 --> 00:15:34,620
来观察当他们驱动掌握自控的
when they flex the mental muscles

291
00:15:34,730 --> 00:15:36,290
精神力量时的大脑。
that govern self-control.

292
00:15:36,400 --> 00:15:38,330
你会听到嗡嗡声。
You're going to hear some buzzing.

293
00:15:38,440 --> 00:15:42,960
今天，David和Steven首次对
Today, David and Steven are conducting their first-ever test

294
00:15:43,080 --> 00:15:44,800
一名罪犯进行测试，
on a criminal offender,

295
00:15:44,910 --> 00:15:47,070
这个罪犯吸毒成瘾，
a man whose cocaine addiction

296
00:15:47,180 --> 00:15:50,740
以致于从他的朋友和家人那里偷东西。
led him to steal from his friends and family.

297
00:15:50,850 --> 00:15:52,970
现在，这位先生要做的是
Right now, what this gentleman is doing

298
00:15:53,090 --> 00:15:55,780
观看一些表现使用毒品的图片，
is he's looking at images of drug-use cues,

299
00:15:55,890 --> 00:15:58,980
然后我们让他选择是增强吸毒的欲望，
and we're asking him to either enhance his craving

300
00:15:59,090 --> 00:16:01,230
还是抑制这种欲望。
to these cues or suppress them.

301
00:16:04,200 --> 00:16:07,090
当他看到使用毒品的画面时，
When the addict sees images of drug use,

302
00:16:07,200 --> 00:16:09,690
他想要毒品的欲望达到峰值，
his own craving for drugs spikes.

303
00:16:09,800 --> 00:16:13,830
功能磁共振成像扫描仪(fMRI)发现他的大脑活性增强，
The fMRI scanner sees this increased activity in the brain

304
00:16:13,940 --> 00:16:16,930
并在一根指示条上显示。
and displays it as a measurement on a bar.

305
00:16:17,040 --> 00:16:21,200
当他脑中的欲望增强，
When the craving networks in his brain are revving high,

306
00:16:21,310 --> 00:16:23,140
指示条移向红色区域，
the bar moves to the red,

307
00:16:23,250 --> 00:16:26,270
如果他克制危险的欲望，
but when he fights his dangerous urges,

308
00:16:26,390 --> 00:16:29,510
他就可以把指示条移到蓝色区域。
he can push the bar back toward the blue.

309
00:16:29,620 --> 00:16:31,780
通过这种生物反馈，
With this bio-feedback,

310
00:16:31,890 --> 00:16:36,620
他就能够训练自己的大脑抵抗诱惑。
he's able to train his brain to resist his impulses.

311
00:16:36,730 --> 00:16:39,320
他做的很好，说真的...
He's doing great. I mean, he's actually...

312
00:16:39,430 --> 00:16:41,190
最终，David和Steven
Eventually, David and Steven

313
00:16:41,300 --> 00:16:43,830
希望能够将这种技术应用于监狱
hope to take this technology to prisons

314
00:16:43,940 --> 00:16:46,830
来帮助罪犯防止再次犯罪。
to try to help criminals not repeat their mistakes.

315
00:16:46,940 --> 00:16:49,030
前额叶健身房的美妙之处
The beauty about the prefrontal gym

316
00:16:49,150 --> 00:16:51,300
在于人们是在帮助自己，
is that people are helping themselves.

317
00:16:51,410 --> 00:16:54,600
如果他们选择加强自己长期决策的制定，
If they choose to strengthen their long-term decision making,

318
00:16:54,720 --> 00:16:56,670
这种方法就可以实现。
this is the way they can do that.

319
00:16:56,790 --> 00:16:59,210
这完全不会改变人格精神，
It doesn't change anything about the person,

320
00:16:59,320 --> 00:17:01,510
而是给他们自己营造更好的机会
it just gives them a better opportunity

321
00:17:01,620 --> 00:17:03,590
来制定长期决策。
to make good long-term decisions.

322
00:17:05,290 --> 00:17:07,250
但是这项技术
But there are some

323
00:17:07,360 --> 00:17:11,220
可能永远不能应用的人就是--精神病人。
for whom this technique may never work -- psychopaths.

324
00:17:11,330 --> 00:17:13,360
他们既可以像正常人一样，
They can pass for normal,

325
00:17:13,470 --> 00:17:17,430
但又会做出邪恶的恐怖行为。
but they are capable of terrifying acts of evil.

326
00:17:17,540 --> 00:17:21,900
很快，一项脑科学的革命会给我们带来工具，
Soon, a revolution in brain science may give us the tools

327
00:17:22,010 --> 00:17:26,080
使我们能在犯罪前发现邪恶的想法。
to spot evil brains before they ever commit a crime.

328
00:17:27,050 --> 00:17:29,210
精神病患者可以对别人实施
Psychopaths can inflict physical

329
00:17:29,320 --> 00:17:31,440
生理或心理上的伤害，
and psychological harm on others

330
00:17:31,550 --> 00:17:34,190
他们自己却感觉不到愧疚。
without feeling a shred of remorse.

331
00:17:34,980 --> 00:17:38,670
大多数人认为他们是邪恶的，
They are the people most of us consider evil,

332
00:17:38,780 --> 00:17:41,890
事实上有精神问题的人远比你想象得多--
and there are more of them than you might suspect --

333
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,300
占了人类数量的3%。
up to 3% of the population.

334
00:17:45,080 --> 00:17:47,570
这意味着世界上有很多危险的想法。
That's a lot of dangerous minds.

335
00:17:47,690 --> 00:17:50,740
但是如果你是其中之一呢？
What if one of them was yours?

336
00:17:50,860 --> 00:17:55,080
假如你的脑部出现了问题，
If something was wrong with your brain,

337
00:17:55,200 --> 00:17:57,250
你又如何知道？
how would you know?

338
00:17:57,360 --> 00:18:01,320
如果让Jim Fallon看看你的脑袋，
If Jim Fallon got a look inside your head,

339
00:18:01,430 --> 00:18:03,160
他就会告诉你。
he could tell you.

340
00:18:03,270 --> 00:18:06,860
他的专业就是研究脑解剖学，
He has spent his career studying the anatomy of the brain,

341
00:18:06,970 --> 00:18:09,560
而重点是研究精神变态杀手。
with an emphasis on psychopathic killers.

342
00:18:09,680 --> 00:18:10,930
六年前，
Six years ago,

343
00:18:11,040 --> 00:18:13,100
两个我的精神病学同事
two of my colleagues in psychiatry

344
00:18:13,210 --> 00:18:15,600
带给我了一大堆这些扫描结果，
brought me a whole bunch of these scans.

345
00:18:15,720 --> 00:18:18,370
我们做了正电子发射计算机
断层扫描(PET scans)，也做了些fMRI扫描，
So we're doing PET scans but also some fMRI's,

346
00:18:18,480 --> 00:18:20,540
通过3/4的这些结果，
and about 3/4 of the way through,

347
00:18:20,650 --> 00:18:22,680
我注意到了一个很明显的模式，
I notice a very definite pattern.

348
00:18:22,790 --> 00:18:26,180
结果表明这些模式都来自于一些非常坏的杀手--
And it turns out that these were scans of really bad killers --

349
00:18:26,290 --> 00:18:28,950
连环杀手，以及暴力残忍的杀手。
serial killers, and very violent killers.

350
00:18:29,060 --> 00:18:32,720
Jim已经鉴别出精神变态谋杀犯人的
Jim has identified the unique brain structure

351
00:18:32,830 --> 00:18:34,550
独特的脑部结构。
of psychopathic murderers.

352
00:18:34,670 --> 00:18:37,220
这是脑的几个部分--
Here are the areas of the brain --

353
00:18:37,340 --> 00:18:39,860
杏仁体、前颞叶、
amygdala, anterior temporal lobe,

354
00:18:39,970 --> 00:18:42,960
眶皮质、内侧前额叶皮层 、
orbital cortex, medial prefrontal cortex,

355
00:18:43,070 --> 00:18:46,030
扣带皮层，然后回到这里的海马体，
cingulate, back here to the hippocampus,

356
00:18:46,140 --> 00:18:48,000
到这下面--看，这形成了一个大的环，
back down -- see, it makes a big loop.

357
00:18:48,110 --> 00:18:50,670
这些区域在精神变态患者中关闭了。
These are the areas that are turned off in psychopaths.

358
00:18:50,780 --> 00:18:56,770
我们脑部结构可以从根本上影响我们如何看世界，
Our brain anatomy radically affects how we see the world.

359
00:18:56,890 --> 00:18:58,810
一个正常人看世界，
How a normal person would see the world,

360
00:18:58,920 --> 00:19:01,010
就像开着这部车，
it would be like driving around in this car.

361
00:19:01,130 --> 00:19:03,620
一个正常人会看他开的速度，
A normal person would be watching their speed.

362
00:19:03,730 --> 00:19:06,780
会把自己置于他人的角度考虑问题，
They would be putting themselves in other people's shoes.

363
00:19:06,900 --> 00:19:09,590
你会开多快？如果你车里有孩子呢？
How fast would you go? What if you had kids here?

364
00:19:09,700 --> 00:19:10,990
你会看着周围的人，
And you'd be looking at people,

365
00:19:11,100 --> 00:19:13,140
人们也在考虑你，这就没什么好隐藏的。
they'd be looking at you, nothing to hide.

366
00:19:23,310 --> 00:19:27,970
而精神变态患者的思维是不一样的，
The world of the psychopathic mind is just quite different.

367
00:19:28,090 --> 00:19:32,210
他们就像在晚上开着这部黑色的车，
It's like driving around in this dark car at night.

368
00:19:32,320 --> 00:19:36,830
防止着路人看到真正的自己。
Now I'm protected from people seeing who I really am.

369
00:19:40,100 --> 00:19:41,420
对于精神病人来说，
As a psychopath,

370
00:19:41,530 --> 00:19:45,290
他们往外看，看到的仅是一些行走模式的物体，
one would look out and you'd see these forms walking around,

371
00:19:45,400 --> 00:19:47,290
这些行人不再是人类。
and they're no longer people.

372
00:19:47,400 --> 00:19:49,390
这样的话，
And so, in this way, you know,

373
00:19:49,510 --> 00:19:52,260
精神病人会利用夜晚，
the psychopath is able to use the night.

374
00:19:52,380 --> 00:19:54,900
这种夜晚，就是断绝对别人的
That is, the night of not connecting

375
00:19:55,010 --> 00:19:58,000
移情作用和感情，
with empathy and emotion with other people,

376
00:19:58,110 --> 00:20:00,170
把别人看做使用的物体，
but seeing them as objects to use

377
00:20:00,280 --> 00:20:03,370
如果有人挡在了道路上，他们就会直接压过去。
and to, if they get in the way, just run them over.

378
00:20:03,490 --> 00:20:06,910
Jim检测了40多个基因，
Jim estimates that at least 40 genes

379
00:20:07,020 --> 00:20:10,650
都和反社会人格障碍
contribute to anti-social personality disorders

380
00:20:10,760 --> 00:20:13,120
和精神病人的大脑运行模式相关。
and psychopathic brain patterns.

381
00:20:13,230 --> 00:20:16,660
这些基因影响你什么时候是暴力的，
These genes influence whether you're violent,

382
00:20:16,770 --> 00:20:18,820
自恋的，或是有杀人倾向的。
narcissistic, or homicidal.

383
00:20:18,940 --> 00:20:22,090
那么如果你具有杀手的这些基因，
So, if you have the genetics of a killer

384
00:20:22,210 --> 00:20:24,960
杀手的大脑结构，
and the brain anatomy of a killer,

385
00:20:25,080 --> 00:20:27,930
就注定会变成杀人狂吗？
are you destined to become a killer?

386
00:20:28,050 --> 00:20:29,370
对于Jim Fallon来说，
For Jim Fallon,

387
00:20:29,480 --> 00:20:33,610
这就关系到不太舒服的个人问题，
this question was about to become uncomfortably personal.

388
00:20:33,720 --> 00:20:36,410
由于担心阿尔茨海默病(老年痴呆)，
Worried about Alzheimer's disease,

389
00:20:36,520 --> 00:20:40,280
他决定对他全家进行脑部扫描。
he decided to run brain scans on his entire family.

390
00:20:40,390 --> 00:20:44,110
所有的测试结果都还好--除了一个。
All of the tests came back fine -- except for one.

391
00:20:44,230 --> 00:20:48,350
那个时候我正好在比较杀手的大脑结构，
So I was comparing at that time all these brains of killers,

392
00:20:48,460 --> 00:20:52,420
我桌子上有我正在分析的资料，
and I had these sheets that I was analyzing on my desk,

393
00:20:52,530 --> 00:20:54,720
我想可能是把这些资料弄混了，
and I thought they had gotten mixed up.

394
00:20:54,840 --> 00:20:57,360
所以我以为家人中这个人的结果
That is, I thought one of our family's patterns

395
00:20:57,470 --> 00:20:58,390
可能和谋杀犯的结果混在一起了，
was mixed up with the murderers',

396
00:20:58,510 --> 00:21:00,130
因为这个结果看起来和谋杀犯的大脑一样，
'cause it looked just like the murderers' brains.

397
00:21:00,240 --> 00:21:02,700
而最后...这个结果是我的。
And, of course, it turned out to be my brain.

398
00:21:02,810 --> 00:21:07,200
Jim的脑部检测结果正表明了他的脑部
Jim's brain showed the telltale psychopathic coldness

399
00:21:07,320 --> 00:21:10,470
在杏仁体和眶皮质区域出现了精神病人的冷区。
around the amygdala and the orbital cortex.

400
00:21:10,590 --> 00:21:14,110
第一眼看到这个时候，实际上我感觉很可笑，
When I first saw this, I actually just kind of laughed.

401
00:21:14,220 --> 00:21:16,710
我拿起它，觉得这个很滑稽--
You know, I took it as like it was funny --

402
00:21:16,830 --> 00:21:18,080
其实是对其有些否认。
a little bit in denial.

403
00:21:18,200 --> 00:21:19,850
这也让我有点迷茫，
And it was a little confusing,

404
00:21:19,960 --> 00:21:22,090
但是我认为我很好。
but I thought I took it pretty well.

405
00:21:22,200 --> 00:21:23,350
接下来，
Next,

406
00:21:23,470 --> 00:21:27,460
Jim分析了他的基因信息和家族史，
Jim analyzed his genetic profile and family history.

407
00:21:27,570 --> 00:21:31,830
他发现他具有遗传性的几十个高风险基因，
He found that he had inherited dozens of high-risk genes

408
00:21:31,940 --> 00:21:35,910
或许他的祖先里就有谋杀犯。
and had ancestors who had been convicted of murder.

409
00:21:42,190 --> 00:21:45,140
然后他询问了他的家人和朋友，
Then he asked his family and friends

410
00:21:45,260 --> 00:21:46,740
他是否表现出了精神病人的症状。
if he showed psychopathic traits.

411
00:21:46,860 --> 00:21:49,010
他们说，“Jim，我们都知道
They said, "Well, Jim, we've known all along

412
00:21:49,130 --> 00:21:51,520
“你是个精神病人--只是你不伤害别人，
"you're a psychopath -- you just don't really hurt anybody.

413
00:21:51,630 --> 00:21:53,920
“你研究每个人的大脑，你影响人们，
"You play with everybody's head, you manipulate people,

414
00:21:54,030 --> 00:21:56,120
你非常有竞争力，你会赢得一切。”
you're too competitive, you got to win everything."

415
00:21:56,230 --> 00:21:57,250
你知道的，所有这类的话。
You know, all this stuff.

416
00:21:57,370 --> 00:21:59,090
他们说，“但是，你知道，你很有趣，
They said, "but, you know, but you're funny,

417
00:21:59,200 --> 00:22:00,830
“而且，你不打人，
"and, you know, you don't swing at people,

418
00:22:00,940 --> 00:22:02,830
“你不做任何伤害别的事，所以我们就没管，
"you don't do any of that, so we just let it go,

419
00:22:02,940 --> 00:22:04,600
不过每个人都知道你是精神病患者。”
but everybody knows you're a psychopath."

420
00:22:04,710 --> 00:22:08,200
那么到底哪个才是真正的Jim？
So which is the real Jim?

421
00:22:08,310 --> 00:22:12,370
是受人尊敬的科学家或参加聚会的普通人，
The esteemed scientist and life of the party,

422
00:22:12,480 --> 00:22:15,570
还是脑部扫描结果显示的危险分子？
or the dangerous man revealed by the brain scans?

423
00:22:15,690 --> 00:22:19,380
我觉得我非常了解自己，
I kind of thought I really knew myself,

424
00:22:19,490 --> 00:22:20,710
于是我变得很自信--
and so I became very confident --

425
00:22:20,830 --> 00:22:22,150
我被人脑所吸引，
that I was interested in the brain,

426
00:22:22,260 --> 00:22:23,880
我研究它，感到信心满满。
I was studying it, I felt confident in myself.

427
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:27,650
这次事件发生的时候，我已经60岁了，
When this happened, you know, when I was 60,

428
00:22:27,770 --> 00:22:29,220
说实话，这对我是个刺激，
that was a shock, actually,

429
00:22:29,330 --> 00:22:32,490
而最后我认为我并不是想象的那样。
when I finally accepted that I wasn't who I thought I was.

430
00:22:32,600 --> 00:22:36,130
Jim发现了令他不安的事实，
Jim discovered an unsettling truth,

431
00:22:36,240 --> 00:22:38,560
但这却留下了一个谜团。
but he was left with a mystery.

432
00:22:38,680 --> 00:22:42,070
如果他有着属于杀手的大脑和基因，
If he has the brain and genes of a killer,

433
00:22:42,180 --> 00:22:44,140
那么他又为什么不是杀手？
why isn't he a killer?

434
00:22:44,250 --> 00:22:47,170
所有的这些事实，潜在的基因风险，
But given all the, you know, the genetic risk factors

435
00:22:47,290 --> 00:22:50,040
以及正如扫描结果表现的出现冷区的
and how my brain is, you know, where it's kind of stuck,

436
00:22:50,150 --> 00:22:51,150
我的脑部结构，
as it were,

437
00:22:51,260 --> 00:22:54,810
这就像是我躲开了一发子弹，
I look like I really dodged a bullet.

438
00:22:54,930 --> 00:22:56,620
这是由于我的父母、
And it was because my parents

439
00:22:56,730 --> 00:22:59,420
我的叔叔婶婶，以及我的祖辈们，
and my aunts and my uncles and my grandparents

440
00:22:59,530 --> 00:23:02,890
是他们使我一直很开心，这是能确定的。
are the people who really kept me happy, that's for sure.

441
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:04,660
说到“本性/培养”--
Talk about nature/nurture --

442
00:23:04,770 --> 00:23:07,690
我就是经历了积极的
that's when nature/nurture was really happening,

443
00:23:07,810 --> 00:23:09,310
“本性/培养”。
in a very positive way.

444
00:23:10,980 --> 00:23:14,670
高风险基因和不一般的脑部结构
High-risk genes and unusual brain architecture

445
00:23:14,780 --> 00:23:17,340
不会自发的制造杀手，
do not automatically create killers.

446
00:23:17,450 --> 00:23:20,510
儿童时期受到虐待似乎是一个很重要的因素，
Childhood abuse seems to be a critical ingredient.

447
00:23:20,620 --> 00:23:23,010
一个充满爱的家庭使Jim Fallon
A loving home helped Jim Fallon

448
00:23:23,120 --> 00:23:25,510
成为了活跃的成功者，
become a boisterous overachiever,

449
00:23:25,620 --> 00:23:28,280
而不是一个危险的精神病人。
not a dangerous psychopath.

450
00:23:28,390 --> 00:23:31,780
不是所有的精神病人都是暴力罪犯，
Not all psychopaths are violent criminals,

451
00:23:31,900 --> 00:23:35,250
但如果他们是的话，我们要怎么做？
but what do we do with the ones who are?

452
00:23:35,370 --> 00:23:38,420
我们仅是单纯的把他们从社会中抹除，
Do we simply remove them from society

453
00:23:38,540 --> 00:23:40,390
不给他们任何机会么？
and throw away the key?

454
00:23:40,510 --> 00:23:42,430
也许我们不需要这样。
Perhaps not.

455
00:23:42,540 --> 00:23:47,400
研究人员没有使用激进的方式消除邪恶--
Researchers are not pioneering a radical way to eliminate evil --

456
00:23:47,510 --> 00:23:51,750
比如直接把邪恶驱逐出大脑。
by literally zapping it out of your brain.

457
00:23:54,310 --> 00:23:58,280
一些宗教认为人类是一种邪恶的生物，
Some religions hold that man is a creature of evil.

458
00:23:58,990 --> 00:24:02,480
我们挣扎着往正确的道路上走，
We may struggle to follow the righteous path,

459
00:24:02,600 --> 00:24:04,720
但最终，我们会失败，
but ultimately, we will fail.

460
00:24:04,830 --> 00:24:07,150
我们全是罪人。
We are all sinners.

461
00:24:07,270 --> 00:24:11,970
但如果我们能让人变好呢？
But what if we could make people good?

462
00:24:17,310 --> 00:24:18,900
在瑞士的苏黎世，
In Zurich, Switzerland,

463
00:24:19,010 --> 00:24:21,640
Christian Ruff博士正在有争议的
Dr. Christian Ruff is blazing a trail

464
00:24:21,750 --> 00:24:24,970
神经科学前沿探索一条道路--
on a controversial frontier of neuroscience --

465
00:24:25,090 --> 00:24:27,980
改变人们的想法和行为。
changing the way people think and behave.

466
00:24:28,090 --> 00:24:30,810
人类的行为在动物界非常独特，
Human behavior is quite unique in the animal world.

467
00:24:30,930 --> 00:24:32,380
与动物不同，
In contrast to us animals,

468
00:24:32,490 --> 00:24:34,850
我们不会只做自己感兴趣的事，
we don't just follow our self interest,

469
00:24:34,960 --> 00:24:37,690
我们可以本着社会准则与规则
but we're able to actually control our behavior

470
00:24:37,800 --> 00:24:40,090
控制自己的行为--
in line with social norms and rules --

471
00:24:40,200 --> 00:24:42,760
这些准则就像我们正在玩的
pretty much like the rules of this card game

472
00:24:42,870 --> 00:24:44,230
扑克的规则。
that we're playing here.

473
00:24:44,340 --> 00:24:47,500
人们总想打破规则，
People are always tempted to break rules,

474
00:24:47,610 --> 00:24:48,930
破坏法律，
to break laws,

475
00:24:49,040 --> 00:24:53,000
而问题就在如果人们开始这么做，
and the problem really is that if some people start doing this,

476
00:24:53,110 --> 00:24:56,310
开始违背社会准则，
if some people start breaking out of social norms,

477
00:24:56,420 --> 00:24:58,350
那么很快就会发生混乱，
then very soon, chaos ensues.

478
00:25:00,660 --> 00:25:03,010
所以在社会中，
So it's quite important for society

479
00:25:03,130 --> 00:25:05,920
使用强有力惩罚的恐吓非常重要--
to put in place strong-punishment threats --

480
00:25:06,030 --> 00:25:09,250
从根本上向人们的大脑中灌输
to basically instill in people's heads the knowledge

481
00:25:09,360 --> 00:25:12,490
如果他们违反准则和法律
that if they violate certain norms, certain laws,

482
00:25:12,600 --> 00:25:14,400
就会受到惩罚这种观念。
then they will get punished.

483
00:25:16,240 --> 00:25:18,900
那么对于没有或不愿意遵守游戏规则的人，
What do we do with people who cannot or will not

484
00:25:19,010 --> 00:25:20,600
我们应该怎么办呢？
follow the rules of the game?

485
00:25:20,710 --> 00:25:25,700
Christian的办法是用电流刺激他们的脑部，
Christian's solution is to zap their brains with electricity.

486
00:25:25,820 --> 00:25:28,370
这些人们通过一个互动电脑游戏
These players are linked together

487
00:25:28,480 --> 00:25:30,510
互相联系。
in an interactive video game.

488
00:25:30,620 --> 00:25:32,280
他们都带着头带，
They all wear headbands

489
00:25:32,390 --> 00:25:35,050
内有电流通过，
designed to pass electrical current

490
00:25:35,160 --> 00:25:38,980
这样可以刺激脑部控制利他的部分，
into the parts of their brains that control altruism,

491
00:25:39,100 --> 00:25:42,720
或是关心他人的部分。
or concern for the well-being of others.

492
00:25:42,830 --> 00:25:45,520
通过使用电流脉冲刺激，
With a targeted electrical pulse,

493
00:25:45,640 --> 00:25:50,360
Christian发现他能使别人，包括他自己，
Christian has found that he can make people, including himself,

494
00:25:50,470 --> 00:25:52,160
变得更体谅别人。
much more considerate.

495
00:25:52,280 --> 00:25:53,500
刚开始，
In the beginning,

496
00:25:53,610 --> 00:25:56,230
贴附头皮的电极下会感到
there's a slight tingling underneath the electrodes

497
00:25:56,350 --> 00:25:58,470
大约30秒的轻微刺痛，
at the scalp for about 30 seconds or so,

498
00:25:58,580 --> 00:26:01,000
